1
00:01:00,690 --> 00:01:08,715
<font color="#3399CC">Subtitles by </font><font color="ffffff">MemoryOnSmells</font>
<font color="#3399CC">http://UKsubtitles.ru.</font>

2
00:01:44,266 --> 00:01:46,626
I'll have to go.
Of course you must go.

3
00:01:46,666 --> 00:01:50,986
Oh, but who'll give the letter
to her ladyship?

4
00:01:51,026 --> 00:01:55,026
Oh, I can do that and I'll take
her breakfast and dress her today,

5
00:01:55,066 --> 00:01:57,986
but I haven't got time
to make it a regular thing.

6
00:01:58,026 --> 00:02:01,426
No, I can do it until she finds
someone else. Lady Mary won't mind.

7
00:02:05,266 --> 00:02:08,306
What's the matter with you?
Miss O'Brien's upped and left!

8
00:02:08,441 --> 00:02:09,761
Never!

9
00:02:12,361 --> 00:02:13,761
Jimmy!

10
00:02:13,801 --> 00:02:15,801
Jimmy. Miss O'Brien's gone!

11
00:02:15,841 --> 00:02:18,041
What? Gone where?
How should I know?

12
00:02:18,081 --> 00:02:21,241
Madge, O'Brien's gone.

13
00:02:22,681 --> 00:02:24,961
- Have you heard?
- Heard what?

14
00:02:25,001 --> 00:02:28,321
- Miss O'Brien? She's gone.
- What do you mean gone?

15
00:02:28,361 --> 00:02:29,721
She's left.
Left?

16
00:02:29,761 --> 00:02:30,961
She's packed up.

17
00:02:42,081 --> 00:02:44,001
I can't believe it.

18
00:02:44,041 --> 00:02:48,081
I can. Sneaking off like a thief
in the night, that's O'Brien to a T.

19
00:02:48,121 --> 00:02:50,561
I just can't believe
Susan has done this to me.

20
00:02:50,601 --> 00:02:54,601
Miss O'Brien did say that she got on
very well with Lady Flintshire

21
00:02:54,641 --> 00:02:56,081
when she was up in Scotland.

22
00:02:56,121 --> 00:02:58,041
So I gather!

23
00:02:58,081 --> 00:03:01,321
"Lady Flintshire has booked
my ticket for India

24
00:03:01,361 --> 00:03:03,521
"and it seems too good
a chance to miss."

25
00:03:05,121 --> 00:03:06,401
Was there really no warning?

26
00:03:06,441 --> 00:03:10,321
Now I think of it,
she'd a telegram yesterday.

27
00:03:10,361 --> 00:03:12,841
That must have been it.

28
00:03:12,881 --> 00:03:14,881
So what happens now?

29
00:03:14,921 --> 00:03:17,081
I'll dress you today, m'lady,

30
00:03:17,121 --> 00:03:21,201
and Anna will take over tomorrow,
until you find a replacement.

31
00:03:23,761 --> 00:03:25,961
And she really gave no clue?

32
00:03:26,001 --> 00:03:28,681
She didn't say anything to me,
Mr Carson, not a word.

33
00:03:28,721 --> 00:03:30,481
As long as she didn't.

34
00:03:32,801 --> 00:03:35,561
I know you all think she must have
said summat, but she didn't.

35
00:03:35,601 --> 00:03:37,561
I believe you. Thousands wouldn't.

36
00:03:37,601 --> 00:03:39,801
She may be my aunt
but she's a dark horse.

37
00:03:39,841 --> 00:03:42,441
No-one will contradict you there.

38
00:03:47,001 --> 00:03:49,641
Susan Flintshire
has stolen Mama's maid

39
00:03:49,681 --> 00:03:51,881
whilst her daughter's
a guest in this house?

40
00:03:51,921 --> 00:03:53,841
I'm sure she wouldn't
put it like that.

41
00:03:53,881 --> 00:03:56,561
I don't care how she puts it.
It's absolutely disgraceful.

42
00:03:58,761 --> 00:04:02,201
It's all right. Madge told me.
It is NOT all right.

43
00:04:02,241 --> 00:04:04,841
Did you have any idea?
No. Not really.

44
00:04:04,881 --> 00:04:09,321
I knew, I knew that Mummy thought
O'Brien was very good at doing hair,

45
00:04:09,361 --> 00:04:11,641
and they talked about her
wanting to travel...

46
00:04:11,681 --> 00:04:14,601
What? When?
When you were all at Duneagle.

47
00:04:15,641 --> 00:04:17,321
And you didn't think to inform us?

48
00:04:17,361 --> 00:04:20,921
I'm in London tomorrow. I can put
an advertisement in The Lady.

49
00:04:20,961 --> 00:04:22,241
Good.

50
00:04:22,281 --> 00:04:25,961
Won't it take forever, waiting for
the magazine to come out?

51
00:04:26,001 --> 00:04:27,641
It can't be helped.

52
00:04:27,681 --> 00:04:29,161
We're meeting Trent at eleven.

53
00:04:29,201 --> 00:04:32,241
I'll see you there. I want to walk
the plantations first.

54
00:04:32,281 --> 00:04:34,761
I was wondering if Mary
might like to come.

55
00:04:34,801 --> 00:04:38,161
Don't bother Mary.
She's got enough on her plate.

56
00:04:40,961 --> 00:04:43,001
Did you really not know anything?

57
00:04:44,321 --> 00:04:46,321
I never thought
she'd go through with it.

58
00:04:51,201 --> 00:04:53,841
I thought you might like
to take this down with you.

59
00:04:53,881 --> 00:04:56,481
In case you want a walk.
It's quite chilly.

60
00:04:56,521 --> 00:04:57,961
Where's the black one?

61
00:05:05,241 --> 00:05:08,001
I'm taking Master George out
for some air, m'lady,

62
00:05:08,041 --> 00:05:10,121
and I wondered if you'd
like to come with us.

63
00:05:10,161 --> 00:05:13,681
I don't think so, but thank you.

64
00:05:13,721 --> 00:05:15,841
Poor little orphan.

65
00:05:18,081 --> 00:05:19,801
Thank you, Nanny.

66
00:05:25,601 --> 00:05:28,321
He's not an orphan.
He's got his mother.

67
00:05:28,361 --> 00:05:29,961
Orphans haven't.

68
00:05:30,001 --> 00:05:32,281
He isn't poor either, come to that.

69
00:05:34,641 --> 00:05:37,801
And you don't mind
my seeing to her ladyship?

70
00:05:37,841 --> 00:05:41,921
Why would I mind? I'm not planning
a trip round the world.

71
00:05:51,681 --> 00:05:55,801
Mr Molesley. Have you seen Mr Travis?

72
00:05:55,841 --> 00:05:59,481
I need to speak to him about
the Bring and Buy Sale.

73
00:05:59,521 --> 00:06:02,281
I'm afraid not, your ladyship.

74
00:06:02,321 --> 00:06:04,241
Never mind.

75
00:06:06,121 --> 00:06:08,601
This were a sad business.

76
00:06:08,641 --> 00:06:10,801
Very, very sad.

77
00:06:10,841 --> 00:06:13,681
I can't believe it's time
for the stone already.

78
00:06:13,721 --> 00:06:15,201
Six months.

79
00:06:15,241 --> 00:06:18,001
They always leave six months
for the grave to settle.

80
00:06:21,121 --> 00:06:24,281
Tell me has, has your son
found another job, yet?

81
00:06:24,321 --> 00:06:25,641
No, m'lady.

82
00:06:25,681 --> 00:06:29,561
They've let him stay on and he
always tries to make himself useful,

83
00:06:29,601 --> 00:06:33,561
but it can't go on forever
and anyway, he's lost his wage.

84
00:06:33,601 --> 00:06:37,121
But he's a properly trained valet!
He could even be a butler.

85
00:06:37,161 --> 00:06:38,801
I hope so, m'lady...

86
00:06:40,841 --> 00:06:43,681
..but it's a changing world.

87
00:06:43,721 --> 00:06:46,041
You don't have to tell me.

88
00:06:54,641 --> 00:06:56,481
Hello.

89
00:06:57,841 --> 00:07:01,201
It'll soon be time for you
to get out and walk, young lady.

90
00:07:01,241 --> 00:07:04,121
Please don't touch the children,
not without my permission.

91
00:07:04,161 --> 00:07:05,321
What?

92
00:07:05,361 --> 00:07:08,281
They are in my charge
and I cannot be too careful.

93
00:07:09,681 --> 00:07:12,801
I would remind you that I knew
this young girl's mother,

94
00:07:12,841 --> 00:07:14,161
which you never did.

95
00:07:14,201 --> 00:07:16,321
That doesn't make you her friend.

96
00:07:16,361 --> 00:07:18,361
As a matter of fact, it does.

97
00:07:18,401 --> 00:07:22,281
Well, I can't stay here
to bandy words.

98
00:07:22,321 --> 00:07:24,001
Will you ask Mrs Patmore

99
00:07:24,041 --> 00:07:26,641
to send up the children's luncheon
in half an hour?

100
00:07:26,681 --> 00:07:29,281
Ask her yourself, why don't you?

101
00:07:35,561 --> 00:07:37,921
Was her ladyship very disappointed?

102
00:07:37,961 --> 00:07:42,241
She was, and it'll take quite
a while to find a replacement.

103
00:07:42,281 --> 00:07:44,881
I can't believe Miss O'Brien
would be so thoughtless.

104
00:07:44,921 --> 00:07:46,721
Can't you? I can.

105
00:07:46,761 --> 00:07:49,001
She wants an adventure.
What's wrong with that?

106
00:07:49,041 --> 00:07:50,441
I don't blame her.

107
00:07:50,481 --> 00:07:53,361
Not for wanting to go, but why
run off and leave us in the lurch?

108
00:07:53,401 --> 00:07:56,561
You're talking like that
so we'll think you didn't know.

109
00:07:56,601 --> 00:07:58,281
That's not kind!

110
00:07:58,321 --> 00:08:00,921
What's the good of a month
of sour looks? Good luck to her.

111
00:08:00,961 --> 00:08:03,641
How was Lady Mary this morning?
The same.

112
00:08:03,681 --> 00:08:06,521
But, I suppose she must
come out of it eventually.

113
00:08:06,561 --> 00:08:08,961
For Master George's sake,
if for no other reason.

114
00:08:09,001 --> 00:08:12,641
I hope so. Right now, she prefers
to leave it all to Nanny West.

115
00:08:12,681 --> 00:08:14,361
I couldn't be a nanny.

116
00:08:14,401 --> 00:08:17,481
Why not? Don't you like children?
No, I do.

117
00:08:17,521 --> 00:08:20,841
Being a nanny you're not one of
the family but not one of us either.

118
00:08:20,881 --> 00:08:23,601
What are you talking about?
Being a nanny.

119
00:08:23,641 --> 00:08:26,321
Daisy was saying she thinks
it must be a lonely life.

120
00:08:26,361 --> 00:08:27,961
I don't know about that.

121
00:08:28,001 --> 00:08:30,841
But if you ask me, that Nanny West
thinks too much of herself.

122
00:08:30,881 --> 00:08:33,841
Why? What's she done?
She always seems nice to me.

123
00:08:33,881 --> 00:08:35,841
She only tried to give me orders.

124
00:08:35,881 --> 00:08:37,961
You mean she mistook you
for a servant?

125
00:08:38,001 --> 00:08:41,521
But he IS a servant. Don't tell him.
He'll never get over the shock!

126
00:08:44,921 --> 00:08:47,001
Thank you, Mr Marsh.

127
00:08:49,041 --> 00:08:51,561
He was bound
to be disappointed.

128
00:08:51,601 --> 00:08:53,841
You're sure you want to
abandon the whole plan?

129
00:08:53,881 --> 00:08:55,561
We don't have a choice.

130
00:08:55,601 --> 00:08:58,161
We have to find the death duties
on half the estate.

131
00:08:58,201 --> 00:09:00,121
They'll be merciless.
I know.

132
00:09:00,161 --> 00:09:03,001
But, I wish we could wait for Mary
to come back into play.

133
00:09:03,041 --> 00:09:04,481
She isn't a player.

134
00:09:04,521 --> 00:09:07,881
She has a life interest in one third
of Matthew's share of Downton,

135
00:09:07,921 --> 00:09:10,001
and a third of his
other possessions,

136
00:09:10,041 --> 00:09:12,401
but everything else
belongs to little George.

137
00:09:12,441 --> 00:09:15,641
And that's all the law gives her?
He should have made a will.

138
00:09:15,681 --> 00:09:17,641
But, Mary's George's guardian.

139
00:09:17,681 --> 00:09:19,881
Surely that gives her
some sort of say?

140
00:09:19,921 --> 00:09:21,401
It's a moot point.

141
00:09:21,441 --> 00:09:23,681
Since I own the other
half of everything,

142
00:09:23,721 --> 00:09:27,241
isn't it more appropriate for me
to manage the boy's fortunes?

143
00:09:27,281 --> 00:09:30,641
Besides, she's in such
a fragile state,

144
00:09:30,681 --> 00:09:33,601
the last thing I want is for her
to start worrying about money.

145
00:09:33,641 --> 00:09:37,081
Of course. But you don't think...?
You've seen her!

146
00:09:37,121 --> 00:09:40,401
She hardly has the energy
to lift a fork to her mouth.

147
00:09:40,441 --> 00:09:42,041
She loved him very much.

148
00:09:42,081 --> 00:09:45,361
And the price of great love is
great misery when one of you dies.

149
00:09:46,961 --> 00:09:49,161
I know that.
Of course you do.

150
00:09:49,201 --> 00:09:51,041
I'm so sorry.

151
00:09:51,081 --> 00:09:53,921
We better get back
or we'll be late for lunch.

152
00:09:59,041 --> 00:10:02,281
I've placed advertisements,
Mr Carson, you know I have.

153
00:10:02,321 --> 00:10:05,681
But the answers weren't suitable?
I didn't get any answers.

154
00:10:05,721 --> 00:10:09,081
Well, I did, but when I wrote back,
I heard nothing more.

155
00:10:09,121 --> 00:10:11,761
Well, I don't know what to suggest.

156
00:10:13,241 --> 00:10:16,641
Because, I can't stay here?

157
00:10:16,681 --> 00:10:21,041
Mr Molesley, I should hate us
to appear inhospitable.

158
00:10:21,081 --> 00:10:22,601
But I can't stay here.

159
00:10:22,641 --> 00:10:26,801
Is it fair on his lordship?
It has been six months.

160
00:10:28,121 --> 00:10:31,801
I suppose I could go to my dad's
until I get something sorted.

161
00:10:31,841 --> 00:10:33,361
What a good idea.

162
00:10:33,401 --> 00:10:36,601
Servants' lunch is on the table.
Thank you, Mrs Patmore.

163
00:10:40,921 --> 00:10:44,641
Oh cheer up, Mr Molesley.
It may never happen.

164
00:10:44,681 --> 00:10:46,361
It already has.

165
00:10:49,081 --> 00:10:52,201
I'm going into Thirsk after lunch.
Do you want to come?

166
00:10:52,241 --> 00:10:55,521
I'm walking down to the village
to see how Isobel's getting on.

167
00:10:55,561 --> 00:10:58,641
Remind her it's an open invitation,
if she wants to see her grandson

168
00:10:58,681 --> 00:11:00,801
or feels like company,
she just has to turn up.

169
00:11:00,841 --> 00:11:02,041
I'll tell her.

170
00:11:02,081 --> 00:11:04,401
I might come with you.
I don't think so.

171
00:11:04,441 --> 00:11:06,801
I don't think she's up
to seeing many people.

172
00:11:06,841 --> 00:11:08,481
I only meant into the village.

173
00:11:08,521 --> 00:11:10,841
Edith, are you really going
to London tomorrow?

174
00:11:10,881 --> 00:11:13,121
I have a couple of errands
if you are.

175
00:11:13,161 --> 00:11:17,081
I'm seeing Michael Gregson.
He must have missed you.

176
00:11:17,121 --> 00:11:21,081
He's giving a party to introduce me
to his literary friends.

177
00:11:21,121 --> 00:11:23,841
How exciting. Isn't it, Robert?

178
00:11:37,041 --> 00:11:40,721
I... I was wondering if I might
put a card in your window.

179
00:11:40,761 --> 00:11:44,201
A card, m'lady?
For a job asking for applicants.

180
00:11:44,241 --> 00:11:47,721
And what sort of job might this be?
Why? Does it make a difference?

181
00:11:47,761 --> 00:11:50,561
No, I suppose not.
Thank you.

182
00:11:50,601 --> 00:11:53,841
Erm, how much will that be?
Sixpence should cover it.

183
00:11:59,401 --> 00:12:02,201
You ought to see more of George.

184
00:12:02,241 --> 00:12:04,161
Poor George.

185
00:12:04,201 --> 00:12:06,961
What a burden he is born into.

186
00:12:07,001 --> 00:12:11,321
A baby rich as Croesus
and a mother almost passed over.

187
00:12:11,361 --> 00:12:13,121
That's the law.

188
00:12:13,161 --> 00:12:15,121
It seems so strange.

189
00:12:16,121 --> 00:12:19,241
Matthew was always so meticulous.

190
00:12:19,281 --> 00:12:23,041
He thought that death
was many years away.

191
00:12:24,041 --> 00:12:26,561
And so it should have been.

192
00:12:26,601 --> 00:12:30,921
If there's anything I can do to help,
please let me do it.

193
00:12:32,521 --> 00:12:33,881
I'm grateful.

194
00:12:39,241 --> 00:12:41,961
But you see,
when your only child dies...

195
00:12:44,561 --> 00:12:46,561
..then you're not a mother any more.

196
00:12:48,121 --> 00:12:49,841
You're not anything, really.

197
00:12:51,601 --> 00:12:53,881
That's what I'm trying
to get used to.

198
00:12:53,921 --> 00:12:55,681
You're a grandmother.

199
00:12:56,881 --> 00:12:59,481
And I know you're going to be
a wonderful one.

200
00:13:09,441 --> 00:13:12,001
Afternoon post.
Ah, thank you.

201
00:13:13,841 --> 00:13:16,321
Oh, not this again.

202
00:13:16,361 --> 00:13:18,161
Not what?

203
00:13:19,641 --> 00:13:22,281
Oh... nothing!

204
00:13:23,441 --> 00:13:25,641
Let me know when
the upholsterer arrives.

205
00:13:25,681 --> 00:13:28,281
I want to be there when you
explain the job in hand.

206
00:13:28,321 --> 00:13:30,361
Very well, but I can easily manage.

207
00:13:30,401 --> 00:13:34,081
He needs to grasp the quality
of the tapestry on the chairs,

208
00:13:34,121 --> 00:13:36,401
before he starts
slamming nails into them.

209
00:13:36,441 --> 00:13:38,641
And I couldn't make him see that?

210
00:14:12,721 --> 00:14:14,761
Just closing, love.

211
00:14:14,801 --> 00:14:17,961
I only wanted to know who was
looking for a lady's maid.

212
00:14:18,001 --> 00:14:20,601
There's not too many in Downton
who need a lady's maid.

213
00:14:20,641 --> 00:14:22,441
You mean the Abbey?

214
00:14:22,481 --> 00:14:24,881
Didn't you used to work there?

215
00:14:24,921 --> 00:14:28,041
I did, but I've been
studying since then.

216
00:14:28,081 --> 00:14:31,841
Studying for what?
To become a lady's maid.

217
00:14:34,617 --> 00:14:39,097
Madge says that Lady Edith
saw Mrs Crawley this afternoon.

218
00:14:40,097 --> 00:14:43,217
How is she?
In a very dark place, I'm afraid.

219
00:14:43,257 --> 00:14:45,137
And no wonder.

220
00:14:45,177 --> 00:14:48,337
For a widowed mother
to lose an only son...

221
00:14:48,377 --> 00:14:51,257
There's no more fit person
to comfort her than you.

222
00:14:51,297 --> 00:14:53,057
I wonder.

223
00:14:53,097 --> 00:14:56,617
If Lady Sybil had been an only child
I believe I'd have died.

224
00:14:56,657 --> 00:15:00,657
No, m'lady.
You would have lived on for the baby.

225
00:15:00,697 --> 00:15:01,977
And so must she.

226
00:15:06,377 --> 00:15:10,297
Thank you so much, Mrs Hughes,
and good night to you.

227
00:15:10,337 --> 00:15:11,937
Good night, m'lady.

228
00:15:11,977 --> 00:15:13,177
M'lord.

229
00:15:16,217 --> 00:15:17,337
Are you all right?

230
00:15:17,377 --> 00:15:21,217
I feel we must settle the question of
who is to manage the baby's property.

231
00:15:21,257 --> 00:15:24,257
I don't want to hurry Mary
before she's ready.

232
00:15:24,297 --> 00:15:28,617
What question? She's George's
mother and his guardian.

233
00:15:28,657 --> 00:15:33,337
Of course she is, but when it comes
to decisions about the estate,

234
00:15:33,377 --> 00:15:34,937
shouldn't it be me?

235
00:15:34,977 --> 00:15:36,257
Why?

236
00:15:36,297 --> 00:15:40,257
Because, together, my grandson and I
own five sixths of Downton.

237
00:15:40,297 --> 00:15:42,137
Mary's share is only for her life.

238
00:15:42,177 --> 00:15:44,457
She couldn't do much with it
even if she wanted to.

239
00:15:45,937 --> 00:15:49,497
The point being that with
the death duties we're facing,

240
00:15:49,537 --> 00:15:51,537
there are some big decisions ahead.

241
00:15:51,577 --> 00:15:53,017
You want to push Mary out.

242
00:15:53,057 --> 00:15:55,617
I'm not pushing her out.
She was never "in."

243
00:15:55,657 --> 00:15:58,657
Matthew was co-owner
and now his son is.

244
00:15:58,697 --> 00:16:03,137
I worked with Matthew.
I must work for his boy.

245
00:16:14,617 --> 00:16:15,897
Thanks.

246
00:16:16,897 --> 00:16:19,457
A lot of letters for a Tuesday.

247
00:16:19,497 --> 00:16:23,057
How do people have time to write
when the week is just beginning?

248
00:16:23,097 --> 00:16:25,177
It's Saint Valentine's Day.

249
00:16:25,217 --> 00:16:28,977
Oh, imagine your remembering that
and my forgetting it.

250
00:16:29,017 --> 00:16:30,777
Who would have thought such a thing?

251
00:16:30,817 --> 00:16:33,217
I am not a complete stranger
to romance

252
00:16:33,257 --> 00:16:34,817
if that's what you're implying.

253
00:16:35,857 --> 00:16:39,417
Maybe I am now, but I wasn't always.

254
00:16:41,377 --> 00:16:44,537
Hold on.
I thought these were all bills.

255
00:16:44,577 --> 00:16:46,817
Daisy! This one's for you.

256
00:16:48,977 --> 00:16:52,377
Daisy? Who's that from?
Don't tease her, Mr Barrow.

257
00:16:55,977 --> 00:16:57,457
Who sent you a card?

258
00:16:57,497 --> 00:17:00,457
I don't know. It's not signed.

259
00:17:00,497 --> 00:17:04,057
Nor's mine.
We both must have secret admirers.

260
00:17:04,097 --> 00:17:06,137
Which is no more than we deserve.

261
00:17:11,697 --> 00:17:15,057
That's enough of that, Mr Bates.
We've work to do.

262
00:17:29,457 --> 00:17:31,177
What's that?

263
00:17:31,217 --> 00:17:33,377
Nothing.

264
00:17:34,937 --> 00:17:37,977
Of course. It's Valentine's Day.

265
00:17:40,217 --> 00:17:43,617
When are you leaving for London?
I'm catching the ten o'clock.

266
00:17:48,497 --> 00:17:50,217
I ought to pack.

267
00:17:52,897 --> 00:17:54,777
Have a happy time.

268
00:18:25,177 --> 00:18:26,817
Just down there.

269
00:18:37,257 --> 00:18:38,937
Mr Grigg?

270
00:18:42,377 --> 00:18:44,057
Do I know you?

271
00:18:49,297 --> 00:18:52,417
You wrote to Charlie Carson
at Downton Abbey.

272
00:18:54,497 --> 00:18:56,257
I work with him.

273
00:18:56,297 --> 00:18:58,337
Did he send you?

274
00:18:58,377 --> 00:19:01,257
Not exactly. In a way.

275
00:19:09,257 --> 00:19:12,577
What... What did he say about me?

276
00:19:12,617 --> 00:19:14,497
What does he plan to do?

277
00:19:14,537 --> 00:19:19,417
Mr Carson is very busy.
He wanted me to find out how you are.

278
00:19:19,457 --> 00:19:22,177
Then, I think
he'll come up with a plan.

279
00:19:22,217 --> 00:19:24,217
I knew it.

280
00:19:25,537 --> 00:19:28,057
He said some harsh things
when we last met,

281
00:19:28,097 --> 00:19:31,577
but, we go back a long way,
Charlie and me.

282
00:19:32,537 --> 00:19:34,417
And whatever's happened,

283
00:19:34,457 --> 00:19:38,577
to theatre folk like us,
that means something.

284
00:19:39,657 --> 00:19:42,497
Yes, I'm sure it does.

285
00:20:11,417 --> 00:20:13,577
Thank you, Madam.
Thank you.

286
00:20:14,417 --> 00:20:16,017
This is very lovely.

287
00:20:16,057 --> 00:20:19,537
Shouldn't you be at the office?
Aunt Rosamund's sending her car.

288
00:20:19,577 --> 00:20:23,497
I've missed you. I haven't stopped
thinking about you for one moment.

289
00:20:23,537 --> 00:20:25,097
Well, I'm here now.

290
00:20:25,137 --> 00:20:28,417
I couldn't be more thrilled
because I've got some news...

291
00:20:28,457 --> 00:20:32,097
Well, not news, more an idea.
Go on.

292
00:20:32,137 --> 00:20:33,417
I've done some research,

293
00:20:33,457 --> 00:20:36,057
and discovered not every country
is the same as England.

294
00:20:36,097 --> 00:20:37,337
You amaze me!

295
00:20:37,377 --> 00:20:40,857
No, I mean there are places where
lunacy is grounds for divorce,

296
00:20:40,897 --> 00:20:44,017
Portugal and Greece
and even Germany.

297
00:20:44,057 --> 00:20:46,777
But, wouldn't you have to live there?

298
00:20:46,817 --> 00:20:49,377
No, no, I'm just finding out
what's involved.

299
00:20:49,417 --> 00:20:53,017
But if I did live in Germany,
would you come with me?

300
00:20:54,857 --> 00:20:56,817
Oh. Hello, Burns.

301
00:20:57,857 --> 00:21:00,257
This is Burns,
Aunt Rosamund's chauffeur.

302
00:21:00,297 --> 00:21:02,217
Good afternoon, m'lady.

303
00:21:04,737 --> 00:21:06,297
Can I give you a lift?

304
00:21:06,337 --> 00:21:08,617
I'll take a taxi.
It's the opposite direction.

305
00:21:08,657 --> 00:21:10,537
I'll see you tonight.

306
00:21:16,657 --> 00:21:19,017
Oh. Am I too early?

307
00:21:19,057 --> 00:21:21,497
No. I'm afraid tea
was a little late.

308
00:21:21,537 --> 00:21:24,697
Oh, dear. Can you manage?
Should I give you a hand?

309
00:21:24,737 --> 00:21:27,017
I can manage, thank you, Miss West.

310
00:21:27,057 --> 00:21:29,097
Come to me, darling.

311
00:21:29,137 --> 00:21:30,617
Hello.

312
00:21:30,657 --> 00:21:35,297
Now, here's the little Prince,
m'lady.

313
00:21:37,697 --> 00:21:41,057
See how happy he is to see his
beautiful Mama?

314
00:21:41,097 --> 00:21:43,017
God bless him.

315
00:21:43,057 --> 00:21:45,457
Thank you, Nanny.

316
00:21:46,977 --> 00:21:50,217
You say you have no purpose,

317
00:21:50,257 --> 00:21:52,817
but what about little George?

318
00:21:52,857 --> 00:21:54,617
Surely you're interested in him?

319
00:21:54,657 --> 00:21:59,017
Of course, but I'm not going to
drive Mary mad by interfering.

320
00:21:59,057 --> 00:22:02,017
It's the job of grandmothers
to interfere.

321
00:22:03,297 --> 00:22:05,497
Oh, that's Molesley.

322
00:22:05,537 --> 00:22:08,697
He asked to see me and I told him
when he could come.

323
00:22:08,737 --> 00:22:10,977
Oh? Well, may I stay?

324
00:22:11,017 --> 00:22:13,577
Or is it a secret assignation?

325
00:22:16,497 --> 00:22:19,577
Oh. I'm very sorry to interrupt
your ladyship.

326
00:22:19,617 --> 00:22:21,377
What is it, Molesley?

327
00:22:22,817 --> 00:22:26,737
I came to ask if you'd consider
giving me my old job back.

328
00:22:28,777 --> 00:22:32,417
I apologise for forcing myself
on a private conversation.

329
00:22:32,457 --> 00:22:33,657
I will go.

330
00:22:33,697 --> 00:22:36,137
No please, m'lady.
There's no mystery.

331
00:22:36,177 --> 00:22:40,697
I've lost my job, because,
well, for obvious reasons

332
00:22:40,737 --> 00:22:43,457
and I thought I'd ask here.

333
00:22:45,857 --> 00:22:49,097
The trouble is,
I have no need for a butler.

334
00:22:49,137 --> 00:22:52,857
These days I'm just an old widow
who eats off a tray.

335
00:22:52,897 --> 00:22:53,937
I see.

336
00:22:53,977 --> 00:22:58,177
Just because you're an old widow,
I see no necessity to eat off a tray.

337
00:22:58,217 --> 00:23:00,057
You and I are different.

338
00:23:00,097 --> 00:23:02,297
Yes, that is very true.

339
00:23:02,337 --> 00:23:03,697
I'm wasting your time.

340
00:23:03,737 --> 00:23:06,377
Should, should we hear
of an opening...

341
00:23:07,377 --> 00:23:08,737
..where would we find you?

342
00:23:08,777 --> 00:23:10,657
I'm moving to my father's house 
today, m'lady.

343
00:23:34,617 --> 00:23:37,777
Have you had any more time
to think about what I said?

344
00:23:37,817 --> 00:23:40,457
You mean our living in sin?

345
00:23:40,497 --> 00:23:43,777
We'd only live in sin as you call it,
until the divorce.

346
00:23:44,977 --> 00:23:46,737
Don't you want to be with me?

347
00:23:47,617 --> 00:23:51,257
You know I do. More than anything.

348
00:23:57,417 --> 00:24:01,297
Erm, is there any more gin, sir?
We're running rather low.

349
00:24:01,337 --> 00:24:03,257
Yes, I'll er, I'll have a look.

350
00:24:27,697 --> 00:24:29,417
Oh, my Lord!

351
00:24:36,177 --> 00:24:39,657
This is an honour, your ladyship.
I have a message for your son.

352
00:24:39,697 --> 00:24:42,977
Lady Shackleton is
joining me for luncheon.

353
00:24:43,017 --> 00:24:44,897
I want him to come and help.

354
00:24:44,937 --> 00:24:47,057
But, what about Mr Spratt?

355
00:24:47,097 --> 00:24:49,497
Well, Spratt will be there,
of course,

356
00:24:49,537 --> 00:24:52,577
but Molesley can still
take an active part.

357
00:24:53,697 --> 00:24:58,337
Lady Shackleton tells me
her butler is retiring.

358
00:24:58,377 --> 00:25:00,737
Won't she have made arrangements
to find another?

359
00:25:00,777 --> 00:25:02,257
But that is the point.

360
00:25:02,297 --> 00:25:05,297
She hasn't yet decided
whether to replace him or not,

361
00:25:05,337 --> 00:25:09,417
so if Molesley can
demonstrate his skills...

362
00:25:10,377 --> 00:25:12,417
Mr Spratt won't mind?

363
00:25:12,457 --> 00:25:14,777
It is not his business to mind.

364
00:25:14,817 --> 00:25:16,617
No. Well...

365
00:25:16,657 --> 00:25:19,337
Very good, m'lady.
Thank you very much.

366
00:25:28,926 --> 00:25:31,546
You took a letter out
of my waste paper basket

367
00:25:31,567 --> 00:25:34,147
when I had clearly crumpled it
and thrown it away?

368
00:25:34,200 --> 00:25:36,960
I could see it had upset you.
I wanted to know why.

369
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:38,240
I'm sure.

370
00:25:38,280 --> 00:25:42,720
But it didn't occur to you it might
not be your concern WHY I was upset?

371
00:25:42,760 --> 00:25:45,560
Well, anyway, I did it and I read it
and I went to see him!

372
00:25:47,720 --> 00:25:49,640
Don't you want to know how he is?

373
00:25:50,520 --> 00:25:53,960
If I wanted to know how he is,
I would have answered his letter.

374
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:56,720
Mr Carson, he's in the workhouse!

375
00:25:59,160 --> 00:26:01,560
And in case you're wondering,

376
00:26:01,600 --> 00:26:05,960
it's as bad as if we were reading
about it in a novel by Dickens.

377
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:07,960
Haven't they closed the workhouses?

378
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:10,760
No. They haven't. Not all of them.

379
00:26:11,920 --> 00:26:15,720
Well, at least he's in the dry.

380
00:26:15,760 --> 00:26:20,280
If you can call it dry when there's
mould in the very air you breathe.

381
00:26:20,320 --> 00:26:23,080
This is a man
you sang and danced with.

382
00:26:23,120 --> 00:26:25,440
Do you feel nothing?

383
00:26:25,480 --> 00:26:28,480
I don't feel I could be helpful, no.

384
00:26:28,520 --> 00:26:30,880
And I would thank you
not to remind me

385
00:26:30,920 --> 00:26:34,080
of a time in my life
I prefer to forget.

386
00:26:35,920 --> 00:26:38,120
But why would we need it?

387
00:26:38,160 --> 00:26:41,560
It's a mixer. It beats eggs
and whips cream and all sorts.

388
00:26:41,600 --> 00:26:43,160
But you and Ivy do that!

389
00:26:43,200 --> 00:26:45,360
And we'd be glad not to,
thank you very much.

390
00:26:45,400 --> 00:26:48,680
Did Lady Edith say why she got it?
Her ladyship asked her to.

391
00:26:48,720 --> 00:26:52,640
She wants to save us a bit of
elbow grease. You don't understand!

392
00:26:52,680 --> 00:26:54,200
Before too long,

393
00:26:54,240 --> 00:26:57,680
her ladyship could run the kitchen
with a woman from the village.

394
00:26:57,720 --> 00:27:02,320
What with these toasters and mixers
and such like, we'd be out of a job.

395
00:27:02,360 --> 00:27:05,680
I want to try it.
Then on your own head be it!

396
00:27:07,920 --> 00:27:10,400
Did you like your card, Ivy?
What's it to you?

397
00:27:10,440 --> 00:27:12,000
That'd be telling.

398
00:27:12,040 --> 00:27:14,080
Don't listen. He's just teasing.

399
00:27:14,120 --> 00:27:18,040
Come on. Clear out, you two.
There's work to be done.

400
00:27:18,080 --> 00:27:20,800
Do you think that means
that Jimmy sent it?

401
00:27:20,840 --> 00:27:22,880
Maybe he thinks it fun to tease.

402
00:27:22,920 --> 00:27:24,640
But, don't you see?

403
00:27:24,680 --> 00:27:27,560
If Jimmy sent mine,
then who sent yours?

404
00:27:33,680 --> 00:27:36,840
Thomas?
Mr Barrow to you.

405
00:27:36,880 --> 00:27:38,600
Can you tell Mrs Patmore

406
00:27:38,640 --> 00:27:41,400
I won't want the scrambled egg
for Miss Sybbie's tea.

407
00:27:41,440 --> 00:27:44,600
If you go through here and down the
stairs, you can tell her yourself.

408
00:27:44,640 --> 00:27:48,400
Do it. Please. I can't stop.
The children are on their own.

409
00:27:49,760 --> 00:27:53,360
Why did I struggle across London
if Rose was going to sort it out?

410
00:27:53,400 --> 00:27:55,040
You had other things to do.

411
00:27:55,920 --> 00:27:59,400
It doesn't explain why Rose put
the card in the Post Office window.

412
00:27:59,440 --> 00:28:01,080
I just thought how much quicker

413
00:28:01,120 --> 00:28:03,440
it would be if we could
find someone local.

414
00:28:03,480 --> 00:28:05,040
I do feel responsible.

415
00:28:05,080 --> 00:28:06,480
Have you had any answers yet?

416
00:28:06,520 --> 00:28:10,280
We've had three and one's promising,
so we'll see her in Ripon on Friday.

417
00:28:10,320 --> 00:28:12,440
Why isn't she coming here?

418
00:28:12,480 --> 00:28:14,840
She's looking after her aunt,
she can't get away.

419
00:28:14,880 --> 00:28:17,160
It's time for you
to come back to us.

420
00:28:17,200 --> 00:28:19,000
What would you suggest?

421
00:28:19,040 --> 00:28:22,600
Take an interest in something,
doesn't matter what.

422
00:28:22,640 --> 00:28:26,680
Poetry... or carpentry.

423
00:28:26,720 --> 00:28:29,920
History or hats.
I'm interested in George.

424
00:28:30,920 --> 00:28:32,400
Are you?

425
00:28:32,440 --> 00:28:33,920
I will be.

426
00:28:35,400 --> 00:28:37,520
What have you been doing today?

427
00:28:37,560 --> 00:28:40,880
Well, it's this whole business
with the repair shop.

428
00:28:40,920 --> 00:28:44,080
Tom, don't bore her
with all that nonsense.

429
00:28:44,120 --> 00:28:46,520
It's my fault. I asked.
Even so.

430
00:28:46,560 --> 00:28:48,600
You concentrate on feeling better.

431
00:28:48,640 --> 00:28:51,440
You mustn't worry about
anything else.

432
00:28:56,640 --> 00:28:58,840
Did you send a Valentine's card?

433
00:28:58,880 --> 00:29:00,080
Suppose I did.

434
00:29:00,120 --> 00:29:02,280
Was it to Ivy?
That's my business.

435
00:29:02,320 --> 00:29:04,720
You didn't really
send Ivy a card?

436
00:29:04,760 --> 00:29:06,240
Is it likely?

437
00:29:06,280 --> 00:29:08,280
I sent one to Lady Anstruther.

438
00:29:08,320 --> 00:29:10,760
I worked for her and
she's coming back from France.

439
00:29:10,800 --> 00:29:12,320
She might be useful.

440
00:29:12,360 --> 00:29:16,920
Wasn't that a bit forward?
Oh, I don't think she'll mind.

441
00:29:18,000 --> 00:29:20,080
To be honest, Mrs Hughes,

442
00:29:20,120 --> 00:29:22,400
I don't see that
it's any of my business.

443
00:29:23,560 --> 00:29:26,520
That's something I never thought
I'd hear you say, ma'am.

444
00:29:27,800 --> 00:29:32,360
A wretched man is in the workhouse
and he reaches out to us for rescue.

445
00:29:32,400 --> 00:29:36,400
Well, he reached out to Carson.
I don't see what you want me to do.

446
00:29:36,440 --> 00:29:38,640
Mrs Crawley, I...

447
00:29:38,680 --> 00:29:40,760
I wondered
if I could bring him here.

448
00:29:40,800 --> 00:29:42,280
Here?

449
00:29:42,320 --> 00:29:45,520
If you and I were to vouch for him
to the authorities,

450
00:29:45,560 --> 00:29:47,880
I'm sure we could get him
away from that place.

451
00:29:47,920 --> 00:29:52,640
But why here? Why not the Abbey?
Isn't he Carson's responsibility?

452
00:29:52,680 --> 00:29:57,760
I'm sorry to say it, but, Mr Carson
has turned his back on his old pal.

453
00:29:57,800 --> 00:29:59,720
I see.

454
00:29:59,760 --> 00:30:04,200
So you want to risk Carson's wrath
by rescuing this Mr Grigg?

455
00:30:04,240 --> 00:30:08,880
He's a pitiful being,
but he's not beyond work.

456
00:30:08,920 --> 00:30:12,880
He's not beyond a decent life,
if he could find it.

457
00:30:14,400 --> 00:30:18,960
You see, in my present state,
I don't think I'm strong enough...

458
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:20,320
But you are, ma'am!

459
00:30:20,360 --> 00:30:23,520
If you could just
set aside your grief,

460
00:30:23,560 --> 00:30:26,280
and use that strength
for another's good.

461
00:30:34,520 --> 00:30:36,680
Mr Branson.
Mr Carson.

462
00:30:38,400 --> 00:30:40,680
I know you won't think it correct,

463
00:30:40,720 --> 00:30:44,000
but I need to ask your help
in a difficult situation.

464
00:30:44,040 --> 00:30:46,960
My help, sir?
Lord Grantham is against me in this.

465
00:30:47,000 --> 00:30:49,160
With Matthew gone,
I've no-one else to turn to.

466
00:30:50,360 --> 00:30:55,120
Mary, that is, Lady Mary,
is not improving.

467
00:30:55,160 --> 00:30:58,360
She's suffered a terrible tragedy.
Of course.

468
00:30:58,400 --> 00:31:00,040
But it's six months now,

469
00:31:00,080 --> 00:31:02,600
and she's no better than she was
a week after he died.

470
00:31:02,640 --> 00:31:06,720
The only way is for her to find
an interest outside of herself,

471
00:31:06,760 --> 00:31:09,640
and I know that should be
in the running of the estate.

472
00:31:09,680 --> 00:31:11,840
What does his lordship think?

473
00:31:11,880 --> 00:31:15,120
Lord Grantham thinks his duty
is to protect Mary and her son

474
00:31:15,160 --> 00:31:16,840
by managing everything himself.

475
00:31:16,880 --> 00:31:19,320
And he won't listen to you
on the subject?

476
00:31:19,360 --> 00:31:21,400
He sees her as a little woman,

477
00:31:21,440 --> 00:31:24,520
who shouldn't be troubled by
anything so harsh as reality.

478
00:31:29,200 --> 00:31:32,880
And even were I to agree with you...

479
00:31:34,080 --> 00:31:35,520
..how could I help?

480
00:31:36,840 --> 00:31:39,840
Give her advice.
She'd take it from you.

481
00:31:39,880 --> 00:31:42,160
What makes you say that?

482
00:31:42,200 --> 00:31:45,400
Because she knows you only
want the best for her.

483
00:31:50,200 --> 00:31:52,560
I'm going down to the pub tonight.
When?

484
00:31:52,600 --> 00:31:55,120
After our supper. I'm bored.
I want to stretch my legs.

485
00:31:55,160 --> 00:31:57,520
You can't. It's not your day off.

486
00:31:57,560 --> 00:31:59,160
I can go for a walk, can't I?

487
00:32:05,120 --> 00:32:08,120
- I could never do that.
- Don't be so soft.

488
00:32:08,160 --> 00:32:11,000
I couldn't. I'd keep thinking
I were going to be electrocuted.

489
00:32:11,040 --> 00:32:13,240
You should have more faith.
More courage!

490
00:32:13,280 --> 00:32:17,200
How about some Dutch courage, Ivy?
Why not come with me, tonight?

491
00:32:17,240 --> 00:32:20,560
What? Me go to a pub?

492
00:32:20,600 --> 00:32:22,880
Without leave? You must be mad.

493
00:32:22,920 --> 00:32:25,240
Don't you ever want to
take a chance in life?

494
00:32:25,280 --> 00:32:26,520
When would we be back?

495
00:32:26,560 --> 00:32:29,400
You're not thinking of going?!
I don't know.

496
00:32:29,440 --> 00:32:31,440
I never do anything
I'm not supposed to.

497
00:32:31,480 --> 00:32:34,120
What are you not supposed to do?
Nothing.

498
00:32:34,160 --> 00:32:37,320
What's going on there?
I'm making the mousse for tonight.

499
00:32:37,360 --> 00:32:40,720
Oh, my God. Well, we'd better have
some soup put by.

500
00:32:40,760 --> 00:32:42,840
I'd rather not rely on
that contraption.

501
00:32:51,480 --> 00:32:54,680
Can I help you?
I doubt you would if you could.

502
00:32:54,720 --> 00:32:56,120
I'm sorry?

503
00:32:56,160 --> 00:33:00,600
Why didn't you give my instructions
to Mrs Patmore about the eggs?

504
00:33:00,640 --> 00:33:03,520
Because I didn't feel like it.

505
00:33:03,560 --> 00:33:07,840
Besides, why can't Miss Sybbie
have an egg to her tea?

506
00:33:07,880 --> 00:33:10,920
I don't have to explain
my decisions to you.

507
00:33:10,960 --> 00:33:13,240
You're a member of staff,

508
00:33:13,280 --> 00:33:16,320
and the orders I give
are to be obeyed.

509
00:33:16,360 --> 00:33:18,160
And aren't you a member of staff?

510
00:33:18,200 --> 00:33:20,160
Not in that way.

511
00:33:20,200 --> 00:33:24,680
Now, I believe I'm needed upstairs.

512
00:33:30,360 --> 00:33:32,320
May I come in?

513
00:33:32,360 --> 00:33:33,880
Carson?

514
00:33:34,960 --> 00:33:36,440
Of course.

515
00:33:38,200 --> 00:33:40,720
I'll leave you now, m'lady.

516
00:33:45,640 --> 00:33:47,720
What can I do for you?

517
00:33:47,760 --> 00:33:50,920
Well, I'm not sure how to start,

518
00:33:50,960 --> 00:33:53,160
but before I do,

519
00:33:53,200 --> 00:33:56,400
you must know that I would only
be as bold as this

520
00:33:56,440 --> 00:33:58,440
if I felt it was for your benefit.

521
00:33:59,280 --> 00:34:01,680
Now you're frightening me.

522
00:34:05,560 --> 00:34:06,920
Your ladyship...

523
00:34:08,040 --> 00:34:10,240
Do you have a moment?
What is it, Barrow?

524
00:34:11,280 --> 00:34:14,000
There's something's worrying me,
m'lady and I...

525
00:34:14,040 --> 00:34:17,000
I can't hold it in any more.
Why? What's happened?

526
00:34:17,040 --> 00:34:18,960
It's Nanny West.

527
00:34:19,000 --> 00:34:22,560
It seems she's been leaving
the children to their own devices.

528
00:34:22,600 --> 00:34:25,160
What? You mean she's
neglecting them?

529
00:34:26,840 --> 00:34:29,680
I wouldn't have spoken up
if it weren't for a...

530
00:34:29,720 --> 00:34:33,280
..a little girl and a baby boy
being put at risk.

531
00:34:37,240 --> 00:34:40,600
Carson, this is my fault.
My lady?

532
00:34:40,640 --> 00:34:42,800
I'm afraid I may have
encouraged you to feel

533
00:34:42,840 --> 00:34:44,920
you have the right
to address me in this way.

534
00:34:44,960 --> 00:34:47,360
His lordship has made a decision

535
00:34:47,400 --> 00:34:50,720
and I find it hard that his butler
should criticise him for it.

536
00:34:50,760 --> 00:34:52,240
No, I never meant...

537
00:34:52,280 --> 00:34:53,840
You do not seem to understand

538
00:34:53,880 --> 00:34:56,480
the effect Mr Crawley's death
has had on me.

539
00:34:56,520 --> 00:35:00,320
As for managing the estate,
I wouldn't know where to start.

540
00:35:00,360 --> 00:35:04,040
But Mr Branson believes you could be
very helpful, my lady,

541
00:35:04,080 --> 00:35:06,080
and as the agent, he should know.

542
00:35:06,120 --> 00:35:09,480
He's just nervous that his lordship
will retreat to his old ways

543
00:35:09,520 --> 00:35:11,800
and abandon all
Mr Matthew's reforms.

544
00:35:11,840 --> 00:35:15,560
And will he?
If he did, wouldn't you approve?

545
00:35:16,440 --> 00:35:18,040
Anyway, whether you approve or not,

546
00:35:18,080 --> 00:35:21,000
I'm sorry you feel entitled
to overstep the mark.

547
00:35:22,560 --> 00:35:23,680
My lady?

548
00:35:23,720 --> 00:35:26,040
We're old friends,

549
00:35:26,080 --> 00:35:30,760
and as I said, I'm sure this lapse
is as much my fault as yours.

550
00:35:32,840 --> 00:35:35,960
But, I suggest we don't
mention it again.

551
00:35:36,000 --> 00:35:38,520
Now, if you'll excuse me,
it's time to go down.

552
00:35:49,080 --> 00:35:51,440
You're letting yourself be defeated,
my lady.

553
00:35:51,480 --> 00:35:55,040
I'm sorry if it's
a "lapse" to say so,

554
00:35:55,080 --> 00:35:57,280
but someone has to.

555
00:36:06,840 --> 00:36:09,480
How is the luncheon
for the tenant farmers coming along?

556
00:36:09,520 --> 00:36:10,920
Well, I think. Tom?

557
00:36:10,960 --> 00:36:14,120
They've all accepted except
for Barnes at Wheelers Wood.

558
00:36:14,160 --> 00:36:17,480
- He's sworn to his sister's wedding.
- I think we can forgive him.

559
00:36:17,520 --> 00:36:20,880
I have a feeling I've double booked.
It's this Saturday, isn't it?

560
00:36:20,920 --> 00:36:22,960
Don't worry.
We're not having the wives.

561
00:36:23,000 --> 00:36:24,200
Edith can preside.

562
00:36:24,240 --> 00:36:27,000
I can't I'm afraid.
I'm going up to London.

563
00:36:27,640 --> 00:36:29,800
Mary, then.
Me?

564
00:36:29,840 --> 00:36:33,160
Well, if George is owner
of half the estate now,

565
00:36:33,200 --> 00:36:34,800
shouldn't you represent him?

566
00:36:34,840 --> 00:36:36,840
Well, I...
I don't want to bother Mary.

567
00:36:36,880 --> 00:36:39,120
They'd like to see you.
I'm sure they would but...

568
00:36:39,160 --> 00:36:42,520
After all, you'll have to run it
if anything happens to Robert.

569
00:36:42,560 --> 00:36:44,800
Until George is of age, or longer.

570
00:36:44,840 --> 00:36:49,320
For heaven's sake! Why does everyone
keep nagging and nagging?

571
00:36:49,360 --> 00:36:53,920
My husband is dead!
Can't you understand what that means?

572
00:36:53,960 --> 00:36:58,000
After all he suffered in the war,
he's killed in a stupid car crash!

573
00:36:59,080 --> 00:37:02,320
Matthew is dead,
50 years before his time!

574
00:37:02,360 --> 00:37:04,160
Isn't that enough
for me to deal with?

575
00:37:04,200 --> 00:37:05,920
Just leave me alone!

576
00:37:08,920 --> 00:37:10,920
Exactly what I was afraid of.

577
00:37:12,480 --> 00:37:14,200
She is living a nightmare.

578
00:37:14,240 --> 00:37:18,000
We must all step back and allow her
to come through it in her own time.

579
00:37:18,040 --> 00:37:22,600
Aren't I right, Mama?
No. We can't discuss it now.

580
00:37:24,280 --> 00:37:28,720
This mousse is delicious, Carson,
is it the work of Mrs Patmore?

581
00:37:28,760 --> 00:37:30,560
I suppose she hasn't bought it in?

582
00:37:30,600 --> 00:37:33,600
I don't think so, your ladyship, no.

583
00:37:33,640 --> 00:37:36,600
Then you must be sure
to send her our compliments.

584
00:37:50,580 --> 00:37:54,780
What's the matter, lad?
Are you not feeling well?

585
00:37:56,220 --> 00:37:58,180
No, it's not that.

586
00:38:00,180 --> 00:38:02,540
Oh, I don't know, Dad. It's just...

587
00:38:04,060 --> 00:38:05,620
It's just lately I...

588
00:38:05,660 --> 00:38:09,380
I can't seem to see
where I'm going, I'm...

589
00:38:10,620 --> 00:38:14,220
You've had a shock, and no wonder.

590
00:38:14,260 --> 00:38:18,380
You should have been working for
Mr Matthew until you were old,

591
00:38:18,420 --> 00:38:20,940
maybe been butler at the Abbey
before you were done.

592
00:38:20,980 --> 00:38:25,020
Now all that's gone,
and your wages with it.

593
00:38:26,660 --> 00:38:29,420
He were a nice chap, Dad.

594
00:38:29,460 --> 00:38:31,860
You know, whatever
the difference between us,

595
00:38:31,900 --> 00:38:33,740
he were always polite, always kind.

596
00:38:33,780 --> 00:38:35,140
I'm glad to hear it.

597
00:38:35,180 --> 00:38:37,980
But, it's raised my standards,
do you see?

598
00:38:38,020 --> 00:38:40,140
I mean, who is this old... bat

599
00:38:40,180 --> 00:38:42,380
her ladyship wants me
to meet on Friday?

600
00:38:42,420 --> 00:38:44,180
Now, listen.

601
00:38:44,220 --> 00:38:48,660
In your game, if you want the best,
you've got to BE the best.

602
00:38:48,700 --> 00:38:50,020
And work at it!

603
00:38:51,140 --> 00:38:54,380
Get yourself back into service,
in a good post...

604
00:38:55,420 --> 00:38:58,380
..and you'll soon find your way
to where you'd like to be.

605
00:39:18,780 --> 00:39:20,740
I'm leaving.

606
00:39:23,420 --> 00:39:26,380
I didn't want to run off
without saying good night.

607
00:39:26,420 --> 00:39:29,700
I suppose you think I behaved
very badly down there.

608
00:39:29,740 --> 00:39:32,740
My dear, I'm not really
very interested

609
00:39:32,780 --> 00:39:35,220
in whether you behaved badly or well.

610
00:39:35,260 --> 00:39:37,740
No?
No.

611
00:39:37,780 --> 00:39:42,180
I am not your governess.
I'm your grandmother.

612
00:39:43,380 --> 00:39:46,900
And the difference is?
The difference is...

613
00:39:49,820 --> 00:39:51,020
I love you.

614
00:39:58,460 --> 00:40:00,300
Of course you do.

615
00:40:01,380 --> 00:40:02,940
I'm sorry.

616
00:40:03,020 --> 00:40:06,460
Mary, you've gone through
a hideous time.

617
00:40:07,340 --> 00:40:10,100
But now you must remember your son.

618
00:40:10,140 --> 00:40:12,740
He needs you... very much.

619
00:40:12,780 --> 00:40:14,500
I know.

620
00:40:15,780 --> 00:40:19,060
The truth is, I don't think
I'm going to be a very good mother.

621
00:40:20,140 --> 00:40:21,380
Why not?

622
00:40:21,420 --> 00:40:25,060
Because somehow,
with Matthew's death,

623
00:40:25,100 --> 00:40:27,460
all the softness that he found in me

624
00:40:27,500 --> 00:40:31,420
seems to have dried up
and drained away.

625
00:40:32,420 --> 00:40:35,780
Maybe it was only ever there
in his imagination.

626
00:40:36,940 --> 00:40:38,740
Now, my dear...

627
00:40:39,780 --> 00:40:42,340
..there's more than
one type of good mother.

628
00:40:44,740 --> 00:40:46,060
The fact is,

629
00:40:46,140 --> 00:40:49,980
you have a straightforward choice
before you.

630
00:40:54,020 --> 00:40:55,780
You must choose...

631
00:40:57,340 --> 00:41:01,220
..either death... or life.

632
00:41:01,260 --> 00:41:04,060
And you think I should choose life?

633
00:41:14,540 --> 00:41:19,260
Charlie Grigg is going
to stay with Mrs Crawley?

634
00:41:19,300 --> 00:41:21,940
The authorities have released him
into her charge.

635
00:41:21,980 --> 00:41:23,620
I'm collecting him on Friday.

636
00:41:23,660 --> 00:41:26,340
But why has she agreed to this?

637
00:41:26,380 --> 00:41:30,140
Because she is a kind woman,
and he is a man in need.

638
00:41:30,180 --> 00:41:34,700
I cannot believe you are imposing
on Mrs Crawley at a time like this,

639
00:41:34,740 --> 00:41:37,420
when she is almost broken by grief.

640
00:41:38,540 --> 00:41:42,420
It's BECAUSE of her grief
I am imposing.

641
00:41:43,460 --> 00:41:46,060
I don't understand you.
No.

642
00:41:47,060 --> 00:41:48,420
You wouldn't.

643
00:41:49,420 --> 00:41:51,620
You must forgive Mary.

644
00:41:51,660 --> 00:41:52,940
I do forgive her.

645
00:41:52,980 --> 00:41:55,140
She is broken and bruised
and it is our job

646
00:41:55,180 --> 00:41:57,580
to wrap her up and keep her
safe from the world.

647
00:41:57,620 --> 00:41:59,740
No, Robert.

648
00:41:59,780 --> 00:42:03,500
It is our job to bring her
back to the world.

649
00:42:03,540 --> 00:42:06,460
I'm afraid that is not how I see it.
Really?

650
00:42:06,500 --> 00:42:11,780
Then I can only say that while I will
overlook Mary's poor judgement,

651
00:42:11,820 --> 00:42:14,740
I find it hard to overlook yours.

652
00:42:14,780 --> 00:42:16,420
Goodnight.

653
00:42:17,460 --> 00:42:21,300
Oh, Edith, Edith,
come to luncheon on Friday.

654
00:42:21,340 --> 00:42:22,900
I...
I mean it.

655
00:42:22,940 --> 00:42:26,460
I need your help
to make things a success.

656
00:42:26,500 --> 00:42:28,260
Why? What are we doing?

657
00:42:28,300 --> 00:42:31,620
We are selling Molesley
to Lady Shackleton.

658
00:42:31,660 --> 00:42:33,460
You mean as a servant?

659
00:42:34,540 --> 00:42:38,260
No. As a Chinese laundryman!

660
00:42:44,580 --> 00:42:47,700
Thank God you're alone.
You've got to help me.

661
00:42:47,740 --> 00:42:49,580
What's the matter with you two?

662
00:42:49,620 --> 00:42:52,460
I don't know, but I'd better
go with him if I want to find out.

663
00:42:57,220 --> 00:43:01,180
Oh, my Lord, what have you done?
She got a bit tiddly down the pub.

664
00:43:01,220 --> 00:43:04,100
She's not tiddly. She's drunk!
He kept buying them.

665
00:43:04,140 --> 00:43:06,660
What were you trying to do?
Nothing.

666
00:43:06,700 --> 00:43:08,620
She's just not used to it.

667
00:43:08,660 --> 00:43:09,980
Come on.

668
00:43:12,420 --> 00:43:15,140
What on earth?!
Ivy's not very well.

669
00:43:15,180 --> 00:43:17,980
That's the understatement
of the year. Bring her inside.

670
00:43:27,340 --> 00:43:30,500
Has Mrs Patmore gone to bed?
She must have, thank goodness.

671
00:43:30,540 --> 00:43:33,220
What were you trying to do?
I wasn't trying to do anything.

672
00:43:33,260 --> 00:43:34,980
As long as you weren't.

673
00:43:36,860 --> 00:43:38,500
Here.

674
00:43:42,660 --> 00:43:45,220
How are you feeling?
A bit better, thank you.

675
00:43:45,260 --> 00:43:46,740
You could have done real damage.

676
00:43:46,780 --> 00:43:50,020
You come along with me now.
What about Mr Bates?

677
00:43:50,060 --> 00:43:52,300
Won't he be missing you?

678
00:43:52,340 --> 00:43:55,300
Mr Bates must wait his turn.

679
00:43:55,340 --> 00:43:57,580
Meant no harm, Ivy, honest.

680
00:43:57,620 --> 00:44:00,060
You won't say anything about this,
will you?

681
00:44:00,100 --> 00:44:01,420
'Course not.

682
00:44:01,460 --> 00:44:02,740
Come on.

683
00:44:02,780 --> 00:44:05,820
It wasn't deliberate, you know.

684
00:44:05,860 --> 00:44:08,660
I know you only pretend
to like her to tease me.

685
00:44:09,780 --> 00:44:12,100
Why don't you tell her
you didn't send that card?

686
00:44:19,740 --> 00:44:23,580
Oh good morning, Mrs Crawley.
I didn't know you were here.

687
00:44:23,620 --> 00:44:25,780
Alfred let me in.

688
00:44:25,820 --> 00:44:28,580
I wanted to see Master George,

689
00:44:28,620 --> 00:44:33,660
but I'm afraid... Nanny West
didn't think it a good time.

690
00:44:33,700 --> 00:44:36,020
Oh, er, Mrs Crawley...

691
00:44:36,060 --> 00:44:39,500
I understand you are offering shelter
to Mr Grigg.

692
00:44:39,540 --> 00:44:41,420
Yes. He's arriving this afternoon.

693
00:44:41,460 --> 00:44:43,580
Only I should hate to feel
you were doing this

694
00:44:43,620 --> 00:44:45,460
out of consideration for me.

695
00:44:45,500 --> 00:44:48,580
Mr Grigg is perfectly capable
of managing his own affairs.

696
00:44:48,620 --> 00:44:51,540
Not very successfully
if he ends up in a workhouse.

697
00:44:51,580 --> 00:44:54,940
I just don't want you to waste
your energy and kindness

698
00:44:54,980 --> 00:44:57,340
on an unworthy recipient.

699
00:44:57,380 --> 00:44:59,620
Not at such a time.

700
00:44:59,660 --> 00:45:00,900
I understand.

701
00:45:01,940 --> 00:45:03,700
But you see, Carson,

702
00:45:03,740 --> 00:45:07,540
I'd almost forgotten I had either
energy or kindness in me.

703
00:45:08,820 --> 00:45:11,140
So that's something, isn't it?

704
00:45:15,940 --> 00:45:19,300
Why is she seeing her in Ripon?
I don't know.

705
00:45:19,340 --> 00:45:21,780
Her ladyship won't wait for
the advertisement to come out?

706
00:45:21,820 --> 00:45:25,380
She will if this one's no good,
but she wants to get on with it.

707
00:45:26,980 --> 00:45:28,620
How are you, Ivy?

708
00:45:29,740 --> 00:45:31,620
Not wonderful.

709
00:45:37,180 --> 00:45:39,460
I suppose we were all young once.

710
00:45:39,500 --> 00:45:42,060
Yes, but you stayed young.

711
00:45:42,100 --> 00:45:44,100
That's the difference.

712
00:45:46,380 --> 00:45:48,460
Forgive me, Miss Braithwaite,

713
00:45:48,500 --> 00:45:51,180
but I have the strangest feeling
I've seen you before.

714
00:45:51,220 --> 00:45:54,020
Well, yes.
I was just coming to that.

715
00:45:54,060 --> 00:45:57,700
You see, I worked at Downton Abbey,
but only for a short time.

716
00:45:57,740 --> 00:45:58,980
Why was that?

717
00:45:59,020 --> 00:46:00,740
I was a housemaid then, m'lady,

718
00:46:00,780 --> 00:46:03,220
and I was starting to feel
it was time to move on.

719
00:46:03,260 --> 00:46:04,300
I see.

720
00:46:04,340 --> 00:46:07,620
So, I took a course in hairdressing,
and I could always sew.

721
00:46:07,660 --> 00:46:09,820
And you got a job as a lady's maid.

722
00:46:09,860 --> 00:46:13,460
I did, m'lady, but it only
lasted for a few months

723
00:46:13,500 --> 00:46:15,820
because the old lady
I was working for died.

724
00:46:15,860 --> 00:46:17,460
I am sorry.

725
00:46:17,500 --> 00:46:20,860
I know I don't sound
very experienced, m'lady,

726
00:46:20,900 --> 00:46:24,900
but I'm a very hard worker and
I've practised what I've learned.

727
00:46:24,940 --> 00:46:28,660
Well, I shall obviously
have to talk to Mrs Hughes

728
00:46:28,700 --> 00:46:30,540
if you've worked for us before.

729
00:46:30,580 --> 00:46:33,420
She gave me a wonderful reference.

730
00:46:33,460 --> 00:46:34,820
When I left.

731
00:46:34,860 --> 00:46:38,700
That does seem very encouraging.
Please say yes, Cousin Cora.

732
00:46:41,380 --> 00:46:43,340
You definitely want the position?

733
00:46:43,380 --> 00:46:46,780
Oh, I do, m'lady.
I enjoyed Downton ever so much.

734
00:46:46,820 --> 00:46:49,740
It was just that the work wasn't
sufficiently demanding.

735
00:46:49,780 --> 00:46:51,340
When would you be able to start?

736
00:46:52,340 --> 00:46:54,300
Whenever you want me, your ladyship.

737
00:46:54,340 --> 00:46:57,580
You can make arrangements
for your aunt? My aunt?

738
00:46:58,300 --> 00:47:01,540
I thought that was why you couldn't
come to Downton for the interview.

739
00:47:01,580 --> 00:47:04,460
I'm sorry. Of course it was.
I'm quite dizzy I've got the job.

740
00:47:04,500 --> 00:47:07,260
Erm, yes,
I can make arrangements for my aunt.

741
00:47:07,300 --> 00:47:10,260
Don't worry about that.
It's settled, then.

742
00:47:17,100 --> 00:47:20,100
I'm perfectly capable
of serving luncheon to three ladies.

743
00:47:20,140 --> 00:47:22,100
So, why are you here?

744
00:47:22,140 --> 00:47:26,580
I've an idea that she wants to
introduce me to Lady Shackleton.

745
00:47:28,300 --> 00:47:30,940
Are you sure this isn't your idea?

746
00:47:32,180 --> 00:47:35,020
I wonder if you haven't pushed
her ladyship into it,

747
00:47:35,060 --> 00:47:37,660
so you could just show off
in front of her.

748
00:47:37,700 --> 00:47:39,540
Certainly not!

749
00:47:39,580 --> 00:47:42,300
I think it's my job you're after!

750
00:47:42,340 --> 00:47:45,500
Nothing to do with Lady Shackleton.
No!

751
00:47:45,540 --> 00:47:47,460
So you say.

752
00:47:51,940 --> 00:47:54,300
Well, I'm going to announce luncheon.

753
00:47:58,660 --> 00:48:01,940
But I'm warning you. That's all.

754
00:48:09,042 --> 00:48:12,682
My son tells me that this decision
to remove the farm subsidies

755
00:48:12,703 --> 00:48:16,603
has dealt a terrible blow to landed
estates up and down the country.

756
00:48:16,640 --> 00:48:18,640
That awful Mr Lloyd George.

757
00:48:18,800 --> 00:48:20,520
I agree.

758
00:48:20,560 --> 00:48:23,680
You know, I sometimes wonder
if he isn't really German,

759
00:48:23,720 --> 00:48:25,720
just pretending to be Welsh.

760
00:48:28,640 --> 00:48:31,680
It's nice to see you here, Molesley.

761
00:48:31,720 --> 00:48:35,760
Molesley's always been a great
supporter of the family.

762
00:48:35,800 --> 00:48:37,080
That's good to hear.

763
00:48:37,120 --> 00:48:39,200
Oh, yes, Granny and I
are always saying

764
00:48:39,240 --> 00:48:41,680
there's no-one more
reliable than Molesley.

765
00:48:41,720 --> 00:48:42,760
No-one.

766
00:48:42,800 --> 00:48:44,360
Careful, Mr Molesley!

767
00:48:44,400 --> 00:48:46,880
Oh? Are you quite well, Molesley?

768
00:48:46,920 --> 00:48:48,600
Yes, your ladyship.

769
00:48:48,640 --> 00:48:50,120
Oh.

770
00:48:50,160 --> 00:48:53,200
What are you doing
in London, Edith dear?

771
00:48:53,240 --> 00:48:55,880
Oh you know, seeing people.

772
00:48:55,920 --> 00:48:58,760
There's no need to shout!
I wasn't doing anything wrong.

773
00:48:58,800 --> 00:49:01,280
It looked to me as if you were
about to drop it.

774
00:49:01,320 --> 00:49:03,440
Well, I wasn't.
Very well.

775
00:49:03,480 --> 00:49:05,960
Here, take this.

776
00:49:08,600 --> 00:49:10,280
Molesley?

777
00:49:10,320 --> 00:49:11,800
I'm sorry, m'lady.

778
00:49:11,840 --> 00:49:14,080
I didn't realise
the plate was so hot.

779
00:49:15,320 --> 00:49:18,160
Poor man. He seems quite new
to this kind of work.

780
00:49:18,200 --> 00:49:19,600
Does he?

781
00:49:19,640 --> 00:49:23,400
Oh, but that is, no dear no,
that is Molesley's strength.

782
00:49:23,440 --> 00:49:26,640
You see he is always
ready for a challenge.

783
00:49:26,680 --> 00:49:29,360
I think it was very kind of you
to allow him to have a go at it.

784
00:49:30,720 --> 00:49:33,200
Yes well, we, we try to do our best.

785
00:49:33,240 --> 00:49:35,800
I do find it very hard these days
to see how many men

786
00:49:35,840 --> 00:49:39,480
are forced to take employment
for which they are quite unsuited.

787
00:49:39,520 --> 00:49:44,040
I hope you'll soon get back to
whatever it is you're trained for.

788
00:50:14,440 --> 00:50:18,240
Come in, Mr Grigg.
Mrs Hughes has told me all about you.

789
00:50:18,280 --> 00:50:20,600
Thank you, Mrs Crawley.

790
00:50:20,640 --> 00:50:22,040
Oh.

791
00:50:23,680 --> 00:50:27,800
I was er... I was wondering
if Charlie might be here.

792
00:50:27,840 --> 00:50:31,440
Charlie Carson.
No. Mr Carson's very busy.

793
00:50:31,480 --> 00:50:34,400
But, I'll tell him
that you've arrived safely.

794
00:50:34,440 --> 00:50:36,280
But, he does know I'm coming here?

795
00:50:36,320 --> 00:50:38,280
We talked of it yesterday.

796
00:50:38,320 --> 00:50:40,080
Now, I've run a bath

797
00:50:40,120 --> 00:50:42,960
and dug out some clean clothes
from the missionary barrel.

798
00:50:43,000 --> 00:50:45,960
They should fit you, at least until
we can find something better.

799
00:50:46,000 --> 00:50:49,920
You're very kind.
Nonsense. It's the least I can do.

800
00:50:54,720 --> 00:50:58,040
While I'm sorting him out, I wondered
if you'd look in at the kitchen

801
00:50:58,080 --> 00:51:01,520
and tell Mrs Field he's arrived
and to put his luncheon on a tray.

802
00:51:01,560 --> 00:51:05,080
I think he should stay in bed today
and then we can see where we are.

803
00:51:05,120 --> 00:51:08,160
He's right. You're very kind.

804
00:51:08,200 --> 00:51:11,080
No, no. We must all do what we can.

805
00:51:38,960 --> 00:51:40,760
Hello.

806
00:51:40,800 --> 00:51:43,440
You look... very glamorous.

807
00:51:43,480 --> 00:51:45,920
I thought I'd make
a bit of an effort.

808
00:51:45,960 --> 00:51:48,000
I'm glad you did.

809
00:51:48,040 --> 00:51:50,200
I've ordered some wine for us.

810
00:51:50,240 --> 00:51:52,880
Heavens, how spoiling.

811
00:51:56,280 --> 00:51:58,480
I love the Criterion.

812
00:52:03,280 --> 00:52:05,400
It feels so wild.

813
00:52:05,440 --> 00:52:10,120
To be out with a man, drinking and
dining in a smart London restaurant.

814
00:52:10,160 --> 00:52:13,120
Can you imagine being allowed
to do anything of the sort

815
00:52:13,160 --> 00:52:14,920
five years ago, never mind ten?

816
00:52:14,960 --> 00:52:17,120
The war changed everything.

817
00:52:17,160 --> 00:52:20,080
Mama used to say we could
never eat anywhere public,

818
00:52:20,120 --> 00:52:22,280
except an hotel we were staying in.

819
00:52:22,320 --> 00:52:25,160
She might cheat and take us to
the Ritz, but that was about it.

820
00:52:26,920 --> 00:52:28,560
I do love you so.

821
00:52:28,600 --> 00:52:30,680
Do you?
Hmm.

822
00:52:31,087 --> 00:52:32,927
I'm glad.

823
00:52:34,127 --> 00:52:38,327
Is that what we're celebrating?
That, and my progress.

824
00:52:40,687 --> 00:52:44,567
I've found out I can
divorce Lizzie in Germany,

825
00:52:44,607 --> 00:52:47,287
if I become a German citizen.

826
00:52:49,756 --> 00:52:53,876
You're willing to become
a German citizen, for me?

827
00:52:54,147 --> 00:52:55,627
You'd do that?

828
00:52:55,667 --> 00:52:58,147
I'd become an Eskimo
if it meant I could marry you.

829
00:52:58,187 --> 00:53:00,707
But... Germany?

830
00:53:02,107 --> 00:53:03,907
After four years of fighting,

831
00:53:03,947 --> 00:53:06,907
you'd join the most hated race
in Europe for me?

832
00:53:14,547 --> 00:53:16,027
Can I kiss you?

833
00:53:16,067 --> 00:53:17,827
What, here?

834
00:53:18,867 --> 00:53:22,227
In front of all these people?
I don't care.

835
00:53:23,387 --> 00:53:26,227
Kiss me. Now.

836
00:53:35,027 --> 00:53:37,187
Edna Braithwaite is coming back?

837
00:53:37,227 --> 00:53:40,307
I would have talked it over
with you first but...

838
00:53:40,347 --> 00:53:42,747
When I saw that wonderful
reference you wrote her.

839
00:53:42,787 --> 00:53:44,867
Yes, but that was because...

840
00:53:46,227 --> 00:53:51,107
I wrote her a good reference because
I thought she was a good worker,

841
00:53:51,147 --> 00:53:54,107
but I don't see her as a lady's maid.

842
00:53:55,867 --> 00:53:58,947
Couldn't you wait for the replies
to the advertisement, m'lady,

843
00:53:58,987 --> 00:54:00,987
before coming to a decision?

844
00:54:02,507 --> 00:54:05,107
I'm surprised at you, Mrs Hughes.

845
00:54:08,187 --> 00:54:11,667
Do you want to prevent my giving a
hard-working woman a helping hand?

846
00:54:20,307 --> 00:54:24,147
You know, it WAS Jimmy
sent that card to Ivy.

847
00:54:25,107 --> 00:54:26,627
You don't know that.

848
00:54:26,667 --> 00:54:28,307
I think I do.

849
00:54:28,347 --> 00:54:32,707
But, if it were him,
then Alfie must have sent mine.

850
00:54:36,267 --> 00:54:37,707
Alfred. Come with me.

851
00:54:37,747 --> 00:54:40,387
Why?
Never you mind. Just come along.

852
00:54:43,947 --> 00:54:47,987
Mr Molesley? Can I get you anything?

853
00:54:48,027 --> 00:54:50,347
A cup of tea?
No. I don't want to be a bother.

854
00:54:50,387 --> 00:54:54,027
How was the famous luncheon?
Lady Edith told me all about it.

855
00:54:54,067 --> 00:54:57,267
Was that before or after?

856
00:54:57,307 --> 00:54:58,307
Before.

857
00:54:58,347 --> 00:55:00,907
Right. Let's just leave it at that,
shall we?

858
00:55:03,307 --> 00:55:05,067
Mrs Hughes was looking for me.

859
00:55:05,107 --> 00:55:07,787
I saw her go into her sitting room.
Shall I fetch her?

860
00:55:07,827 --> 00:55:08,907
No need.

861
00:55:08,947 --> 00:55:10,947
I'm sorry not to have mentioned it.

862
00:55:10,987 --> 00:55:13,227
I sent the card to Ivy.

863
00:55:14,107 --> 00:55:16,427
But... Jimmy said...

864
00:55:16,467 --> 00:55:17,667
He didn't send one.

865
00:55:17,707 --> 00:55:20,907
Or, if he did,
it wasn't to anyone at Downton.

866
00:55:23,587 --> 00:55:25,707
Good night, Mrs Patmore.

867
00:55:33,427 --> 00:55:35,867
If it weren't him,
then who did send my card?

868
00:55:37,547 --> 00:55:39,187
I won't sleep now.

869
00:55:39,227 --> 00:55:41,747
Oh, for heaven's sake! I sent it.

870
00:55:42,707 --> 00:55:44,667
You?!
Yes.

871
00:55:46,307 --> 00:55:49,907
I thought Alfred
would buy one for Ivy,

872
00:55:49,947 --> 00:55:53,027
and I didn't want you to
have nothing to open if he did.

873
00:55:54,667 --> 00:55:56,507
I'm sorry if I did wrong.

874
00:55:56,547 --> 00:55:58,947
No, don't be sorry.

875
00:55:58,987 --> 00:56:02,307
I may not have a follower,
but at least I've got a friend.

876
00:56:04,387 --> 00:56:06,747
Goodnight, Mrs Patmore.

877
00:56:12,227 --> 00:56:16,147
I didn't know what else to say.
I had written her a good reference.

878
00:56:16,187 --> 00:56:17,787
Only because I asked you to.

879
00:56:17,827 --> 00:56:22,067
And was I to tell her that,
Mr Branson, and why you asked me?

880
00:56:22,107 --> 00:56:23,947
It's up to me to tell her.

881
00:56:23,987 --> 00:56:25,867
I'm the one who got us
into this fix.

882
00:56:25,907 --> 00:56:27,507
No.

883
00:56:27,547 --> 00:56:30,387
Her ladyship has lost a daughter
and a son-in-law.

884
00:56:30,427 --> 00:56:34,867
I cannot now let her think that
Lady Sybil's husband was unworthy.

885
00:56:34,907 --> 00:56:36,987
It's too much.

886
00:56:38,227 --> 00:56:40,307
Then what are we to do?

887
00:56:40,347 --> 00:56:42,147
We can't have her here.
We must.

888
00:56:42,187 --> 00:56:46,027
We'll let her come back and we'll
make sure that she behaves herself.

889
00:56:46,067 --> 00:56:47,827
And if she doesn't?

890
00:56:47,867 --> 00:56:50,187
We must keep a firm eye on her,

891
00:56:50,227 --> 00:56:54,267
and I hope we may rely on you to see
that Edna doesn't step out of line.

892
00:56:54,307 --> 00:56:55,987
Of course.

893
00:56:56,027 --> 00:56:59,747
After all, she does appear to have
acquired some proper training.

894
00:56:59,787 --> 00:57:01,787
I don't think that's a lie.

895
00:57:01,827 --> 00:57:04,307
Maybe she's moved
into the real world.

896
00:57:07,187 --> 00:57:10,207
Well, it all sounds like
a ticking bomb to me.

897
00:57:25,406 --> 00:57:29,166
There, there, my precious boy,

898
00:57:29,206 --> 00:57:34,886
and don't let that chauffeur's
daughter disturb you any more.

899
00:57:38,286 --> 00:57:40,166
Go back to sleep,

900
00:57:40,206 --> 00:57:43,686
you wicked little cross-breed.

901
00:57:46,886 --> 00:57:50,206
Your ladyship...
I didn't see you there.

902
00:57:50,246 --> 00:57:51,606
Obviously not.

903
00:57:51,646 --> 00:57:53,366
I was just er...

904
00:57:53,406 --> 00:57:56,026
I was just having a game
with Miss Sybbie.

905
00:57:56,034 --> 00:57:59,514
I want you to pack tonight and
leave first thing in the morning.

906
00:57:59,554 --> 00:58:00,994
But, your ladyship...

907
00:58:01,034 --> 00:58:03,634
Please put Master George
back into his crib.

908
00:58:03,674 --> 00:58:06,274
You are not to touch
the children again.

909
00:58:07,354 --> 00:58:10,194
Oh. I thought it was
Nanny West ringing.

910
00:58:10,234 --> 00:58:13,354
No, Mrs Hughes, Nanny West
is leaving in the morning.

911
00:58:13,394 --> 00:58:15,514
Can you find her a bed for the night

912
00:58:15,554 --> 00:58:18,234
and ask one of the maids
to sleep with the children?

913
00:58:18,274 --> 00:58:21,394
But, your ladyship,
I was only joking.

914
00:58:21,434 --> 00:58:23,034
I prefer not to discuss it,

915
00:58:23,074 --> 00:58:26,594
except to say that your values
have no place in a civilised home!

916
00:58:27,754 --> 00:58:29,354
Now, Mrs Hughes...

917
00:58:31,074 --> 00:58:33,394
I'll wait here
while Nanny West packs.

918
00:58:34,874 --> 00:58:37,474
You will fetch a maid
and prepare a room.

919
00:58:37,514 --> 00:58:41,114
You understand, Miss West is not to
be left alone with the children.

920
00:58:41,154 --> 00:58:42,354
Not for one minute.

921
00:58:44,354 --> 00:58:47,834
I wonder how Edith's getting on.
She has quite a London life.

922
00:58:47,874 --> 00:58:51,154
She's seeing that publisher.
Is it serious?

923
00:58:52,074 --> 00:58:56,114
Well, he's not bad looking
and he's still alive,

924
00:58:56,154 --> 00:58:59,714
which puts him two points ahead
of most men of our generation.

925
00:59:00,794 --> 00:59:03,754
Which doesn't alter the fact
she could do a great deal better.

926
00:59:03,794 --> 00:59:06,954
Do you know anything about him?
Not really.

927
00:59:07,994 --> 00:59:10,474
He talked to Matthew a bit
when we were at Duneagle...

928
00:59:17,234 --> 00:59:18,874
Go to bed.

929
00:59:18,914 --> 00:59:21,234
You look done in.

930
00:59:28,274 --> 00:59:30,994
Do you want me at the tenants'
luncheon tomorrow?

931
00:59:31,034 --> 00:59:33,154
No, there's no reason for you to be.

932
00:59:33,194 --> 00:59:36,714
I know you're trying to shield me...
Let me manage things in my own way.

933
00:59:36,754 --> 00:59:39,114
Please. It'll be for the best.

934
00:59:41,194 --> 00:59:44,834
I have ideas, you know.
Matthew and I used to talk...

935
00:59:44,874 --> 00:59:47,634
My dear, I know I'm right in this.

936
00:59:48,994 --> 00:59:50,794
Now, go to bed.

937
01:00:14,594 --> 01:00:16,034
My lady?

938
01:00:16,074 --> 01:00:18,794
I'm sorry to bother you so late,

939
01:00:18,834 --> 01:00:21,394
but I think you know why I've come.

940
01:00:22,754 --> 01:00:26,114
To apologise.
You have nothing to apologise for.

941
01:00:26,154 --> 01:00:28,994
I pushed into your room
and I spoke impertinently.

942
01:00:29,034 --> 01:00:31,874
I suppose you know my grandmother
agrees with you.

943
01:00:31,914 --> 01:00:34,114
That doesn't surprise me.

944
01:00:35,674 --> 01:00:39,714
So, does this mean you've decided to
return to the land of the living?

945
01:00:41,434 --> 01:00:44,714
Because, if so, I'm glad.

946
01:00:45,794 --> 01:00:50,994
It means that I know that I've spent
too long in the land of the dead.

947
01:00:52,194 --> 01:00:55,354
We were very fond of Mr Crawley,
you know, my lady.

948
01:00:55,394 --> 01:00:57,314
All of us.

949
01:01:01,234 --> 01:01:02,714
I...

950
01:01:20,274 --> 01:01:22,634
You cry, m'lady.

951
01:01:23,634 --> 01:01:26,674
You have a good cry.

952
01:01:26,714 --> 01:01:29,474
That's what's needed now.

953
01:01:31,954 --> 01:01:33,674
And when you're ready...

954
01:01:34,874 --> 01:01:37,234
..you can get to work.

955
01:01:37,274 --> 01:01:39,994
Because you are strong enough.

956
01:01:45,714 --> 01:01:48,474
You're strong enough for the task.

957
01:01:48,514 --> 01:01:50,914
But am I, Carson?

958
01:01:50,954 --> 01:01:54,034
That's the point.
Papa doesn't seem to think so.

959
01:01:54,074 --> 01:01:58,514
Don't you owe it to Mr Crawley,
to protect his work?

960
01:01:58,554 --> 01:02:00,994
To fight for the changes he made?

961
01:02:01,034 --> 01:02:04,074
To steer Downton
in the right direction?

962
01:02:04,114 --> 01:02:06,074
I know I can always count on you

963
01:02:06,114 --> 01:02:09,314
for a draught of self-confidence
whenever I start to doubt.

964
01:02:09,354 --> 01:02:12,434
And you will always find one here.

965
01:02:16,114 --> 01:02:17,794
Good night, Carson.

966
01:02:19,994 --> 01:02:21,634
Good night, m'lady.

967
01:02:32,594 --> 01:02:34,794
Oh my gosh.

968
01:02:38,194 --> 01:02:41,194
Ohh...
What in God's name...?

969
01:02:41,234 --> 01:02:45,114
I must have put those
ruddy prongs in wrong.

970
01:02:45,154 --> 01:02:46,714
You did something wrong.

971
01:02:46,754 --> 01:02:49,554
What a day this has
turned out to be.

972
01:02:49,594 --> 01:02:51,034
Why?

973
01:02:51,074 --> 01:02:53,234
Nanny West has been sacked.

974
01:02:53,274 --> 01:02:55,274
So, that's fun for tomorrow.

975
01:02:55,314 --> 01:02:58,474
Well, I'll get the sack
if anyone sees this!

976
01:02:58,514 --> 01:03:01,314
Let Daisy and Ivy clean it up
in the morning.

977
01:03:01,354 --> 01:03:04,154
But Daisy mustn't find out
I don't know how to work it.

978
01:03:04,194 --> 01:03:05,314
Why ever not?

979
01:03:05,354 --> 01:03:07,234
Because it makes her
part of the future

980
01:03:07,274 --> 01:03:09,714
and leaves me stuck in the past,
don't you see?

981
01:03:12,474 --> 01:03:16,274
Oh... Fetch me an apron.
We'll do it together.

982
01:03:16,314 --> 01:03:19,114
Would you, really?
Why not?

983
01:03:19,154 --> 01:03:20,994
Who needs sleep?

984
01:03:21,674 --> 01:03:22,994
Thank you.

985
01:03:29,274 --> 01:03:32,474
I was never sure I liked her.
Nanny West?

986
01:03:32,514 --> 01:03:34,674
It's not for me to have an opinion,

987
01:03:34,714 --> 01:03:36,874
but I will say this...

988
01:03:54,754 --> 01:03:57,194
Where did you get to last night?

989
01:03:57,234 --> 01:04:01,434
I was fast asleep before you came in
and you were sleeping when I left.

990
01:04:01,474 --> 01:04:03,154
I'm afraid it was very late.

991
01:04:03,194 --> 01:04:06,074
But I'm glad you're here now
because I want you to know

992
01:04:06,114 --> 01:04:10,394
that we owe a great debt
to Barrow, here.

993
01:04:12,114 --> 01:04:15,074
It turns out Miss West is quite
unsuited to the role of Nanny.

994
01:04:15,114 --> 01:04:17,474
She is leaving today.
What? Not another one.

995
01:04:17,514 --> 01:04:20,154
Trust me. We'll talk later.
I must run or I'll be late.

996
01:04:21,674 --> 01:04:25,234
Barrow, I meant what I said.
Thank you.

997
01:04:25,274 --> 01:04:27,354
I'm glad, your ladyship.

998
01:04:27,394 --> 01:04:31,754
I just had a hunch that she
wasn't quite all Sir Garnet.

999
01:04:46,714 --> 01:04:49,154
I've no problem,
as long as you test them first.

1000
01:04:55,034 --> 01:04:56,634
I'm so sorry to miss your arrival.

1001
01:04:56,674 --> 01:04:58,954
That's all right, m'lady.
We've not begun to eat.

1002
01:04:58,994 --> 01:05:02,074
Come and sit here.
I'll manage very well at the end.

1003
01:05:02,114 --> 01:05:03,594
Carson can find me a chair.

1004
01:05:04,394 --> 01:05:07,154
No. Sit here. I'll go to the end.

1005
01:05:09,834 --> 01:05:11,594
This is your place.

1006
01:05:12,474 --> 01:05:15,594
Are you sure you have time for this?
Quite sure.

1007
01:05:15,634 --> 01:05:17,074
I've been looking forward to it.

1008
01:05:18,114 --> 01:05:21,914
Now, Mr Taylor. What's all this
I hear about your giving up sheep?

1009
01:05:21,954 --> 01:05:25,234
Well, m'lady, it's not
quite as simple as that.

1010
01:05:25,274 --> 01:05:29,074
If truth be told,
I'd rather be farming livestock...

1011
01:05:30,314 --> 01:05:31,994
And why is that?

1012
01:05:45,674 --> 01:05:47,674
There's a letter from Matthew.
Where?

1013
01:05:47,714 --> 01:05:49,674
Hidden in a book in the office.

1014
01:05:49,714 --> 01:05:51,554
Are you trying to hide
from the truth?

1015
01:05:51,594 --> 01:05:54,834
That you would prefer to be
in sole charge of the estate.

1016
01:05:54,874 --> 01:05:57,354
There's bad feeling
between Braithwaite and Anna.

1017
01:05:57,394 --> 01:05:59,234
M'lord?
Anna has been a little unkind.

1018
01:05:59,274 --> 01:06:01,194
What's so funny?
Nothing, Mr Bates.

1019
01:06:01,234 --> 01:06:03,914
I want "Tom"
to be your instructor.

1020
01:06:03,954 --> 01:06:05,954
Are we to do all this
without telling Papa?

1021
01:06:05,994 --> 01:06:08,754
There can be too much truth
in any relationship.

1022
01:06:08,794 --> 01:06:11,634
Lady Rose asked me if I'd
chaperone her to a The Dansant.

1023
01:06:11,674 --> 01:06:13,234
Keep her out of trouble.

1024
01:06:13,714 --> 01:06:15,485
Do you want to be arrested?

1025
01:06:15,559 --> 01:06:36,950
